Alright, going section by section, everything I can think of, including the stuff from before.

Title
Too long and reads like an abstract sentence instead of a title. It also front loads a conclusion instead of a question, which is a strange choice since your whole paper is about testing something rather than asserting it. Something shorter that poses the question would pull a reader in faster.

Abstract
It works but it is packed wall to wall with numbers. A reader skimming for the one sentence takeaway has to dig for it. Consider one sentence near the top that states the core finding in plain words before the numbers start, something like: the model looks like it wins on average error, but that win comes almost entirely from predicting collapses to the risk floor, not from genuinely better forecasts. Right now that idea is there but it is diluted across many clauses.

Introduction
The four hypotheses are a strong structural choice, that part is genuinely good. Two things to add. First, give the reader a one line preview of which hypotheses held and which did not, right at the end of the intro. Right now they have to read the whole results section to find out. Second, the opening sentence is a bit flat. You are sitting on a good hook, actual satellites, actual collision risk, actual public agency data, and the intro undersells that.

Methods
This is where the most real gaps are.

You never give a feature list. You mention snapshot fields, history summaries, and covariance trends as ablation steps that move MAE from 2.904 to 2.851 to 2.808, but you never tell the reader what is actually in those buckets. A short table listing feature names and where they come from would answer a question every reviewer will ask.

You mention SHAP explains the fitted residual regressor in the discussion, but you never show it. Either cut the SHAP mention or include the actual output, even as a small figure. Right now it is a claim with no evidence attached.

No hyperparameters are given for XGBoost. Depth, learning rate, number of trees, early stopping rule, anything. This matters for reproducibility even though you do release the freeze seed and rerun protocol.

The notation is a little loose. Risk and y appear before they are formally defined as the pre cutoff snapshot value and the final reported value. A single defining sentence early in Methods would remove any ambiguity for a reader who has not internalized CDM vocabulary yet.

One line in the PDF extraction looks garbled, the yb equals risk plus y minus risk sentence in section III-A. Worth checking your LaTeX source, since that is either an OCR artifact or an actual typo in the equation.

Results
The two most damning and most convincing findings in the whole paper are both under told. The leave one out result, persistence beating your model on 66 out of 66 high risk events, is sitting in the middle of a paragraph in III-A. That is about as clean a piece of evidence as you will ever get that the model should not be trusted near the tail, and it deserves its own sentence or its own line in the abstract.

The unguarded XGBoost loss of 1.75 times 10 to the 6 in Table IV needs narration. As written it looks like a typo. It is actually one of the most important numbers in the paper because it shows a model that looks strong on MAE can be catastrophic on the metric that matters operationally. Explain in one sentence what happened, almost certainly one wildly overconfident prediction on a real high risk event blowing up the numerator.

Sesc appears twice as a comparison number, 0.556, and is never defined. Anyone not deep in the Uriot 2022 paper will not know what it stands for.

Section III-F ends with an honest but soft admission, that the local split does not have enough positives to support a claim at the tighter operational cuts. That is a real limitation and it is currently phrased almost as a footnote. State it more directly, something like: this study cannot currently say whether the model helps or hurts at the cutoffs operators actually use, only at the looser ESA cutoff.

No discussion of the 174 false positives from the floor classifier at threshold 0.15. What do those events look like. Are they clustered near the boundary or scattered. A practitioner reading this paper will want to know what a false floor call costs them.

Figures
Fig 2 only has uncertainty whiskers at the 48 hour point. If you have the five seed redraws at other horizons too, showing bars at all four points would let a reader see whether the horizon effect itself is stable or noisy.

Fig 4 is log scale and the unguarded XGBoost bar towers over everything. Annotate the actual value on the bar itself rather than making the reader go back to the table.

No calibration or reliability plot for the floor classifier probabilities. Given that the classifier threshold of 0.15 is doing a lot of work in your selected policy, a reliability diagram would strengthen trust in that choice.

No predicted versus actual scatter plot anywhere. Given how central the floor collapse behavior is to your story, a scatter with the floor line marked would make the mechanism visually obvious in a way the histogram in Fig 1 only partly does.

Discussion
Good synthesis, but it stays at the level of statistics without translating to what an operator should actually do differently. One paragraph of concrete guidance would help: something like, use this model to triage which reports are safe to deprioritize, not to assess ones that are already flagged high risk.

No mention of compute cost or inference latency anywhere, even though your PRISM repo apparently runs CPU only. That is a relevant practical detail for a paper that is implicitly making a case for real world usefulness of early forecasting.

No discussion of how a 2015 to 2019 dataset generalizes to today's much denser catalog, given how much conjunction volume has grown with mega constellations. You note manoeuvres are out of scope and the data is historical, but you do not discuss whether the floor collapse rate itself might be different in a denser tracking environment now. That is a meaningful limitation given the paper's forward looking framing.

References
Eight references is thin for a paper claiming to unify its results with the existing dilution literature. Citations 2 and 6 are both pre 2020. A couple of more recent works on CDM based forecasting or space situational awareness would strengthen the related work grounding and preempt an obvious reviewer question about why the lit review stops around 2018.

General
No statement of code or data availability. Since your whole argument rests on transparency about a negative result, a line pointing to where the code and frozen splits live would match the spirit of the paper and let others actually check your work.

No correction for multiple comparisons across the many tests you run, horizon sweeps, threshold sweeps, quartile splits. Given the exploratory framing this is probably fine, but a one sentence caveat acknowledging it would preempt a sharp reviewer.

No stated target venue. The polish level, single high school author, and the honest negative result framing suggest this could go several directions, arXiv preprint, a space situational awareness workshop, or a science fair style write up. The right next edit pass depends a lot on which one you are aiming for, since the acceptable density of unexplained numbers is very different across those.

