# 10. THE ABSTENTION ANALYSIS NEEDS TO BECOME A REAL SELECTIVE-PREDICTION ANALYSIS

You currently say:

> accepts 1500 of 1659
> coverage 90.4%
> false reassurance 2 of 9 

Interesting.

But this deserves its own analysis.

You have an abstaining forecaster.

So evaluate:

[
\text{coverage} \leftrightarrow \text{risk of accepted set}
]

As the abstention threshold changes.

Show:

* acceptance rate
* MAE among accepted events
* high-risk recall
* false reassurance
* average interval width
* number of abstentions

That turns “we abstain when the band crosses −6” into an actual **selective prediction study**.

---

# 11. THE HIGH-RISK SAMPLE IS TINY

This is important.

You have:

**66 local high-risk events**

and only:

**9 at −6** in the local test.

The official test has 150 positives. 

Your paper correctly recognizes that high-risk evaluation is hard.

But then you sometimes speak about H3 in a way that sounds broader than the evidence.

Your result is not:

> “ML does not improve high-risk conjunction forecasting.”

It is:

> “Under this dataset and evaluation protocol, these models did not demonstrate improved performance in the high-risk tail.”

That's much safer.

Especially because one of your strongest results comes from leave-one-out over just 66 high-risk events, where persistence wins 66/66. 

That is fascinating, but statistically fragile.

